  "version": "as-filed",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship) not stated.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Const. Art. IV, Sec. 1 (proposed text)\n(quote) Every person, being eighteen years of age, being a citizen of the United States, being a resident of the State for a period of time as prescribed by the General Assembly, and being duly registered in the county of residence for a period of time prior to the day of any election as prescribed by the General Assembly, shall be entitled to vote in all federal, state, and local elections held in the county or district in which such person resides.\n(section) Const. Art. IV, Sec. 1 (proposed text)\n(quote) No person who is not a citizen of the United States shall be entitled to vote in any election held in this state, including elections for state, county, municipal, or district offices, or for any ballot measure, referendum, or other question submitted to the people.\n(other) Keeps the 18-year-old, residency and registration qualifications 'as prescribed by the General Assembly', equal and uniform across the state.\n(other) Keeps the General Assembly's power to require voting in home precincts and to secure 'the purity of the ballot box'.\n(other) Keeps the military-duty sentence for male citizens.\n(other) Final resolving clauses: refer to the 115th General Assembly and publish per Art. XI, Sec. 3.\n(other) version: 3345564-Draft (only saved version): slot e only; same text as HJR0808 apart from the originating-chamber wording and the clerk's clause. No amendments saved or recorded.\n(other) note: Resolution, not a statute: 'enacted' false because it only proposes an amendment; it still needs a later General Assembly and voter ratification. For these votes, id_text_in_voted_version=true means the voted text carries the citizens-only voting language (slot e); it has no ID rule. The House substituted this for HJR0808 (14 April 2026). Draft text only; no amendments saved or recorded.\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
